# Circle Agent Stack
# Authentication is handled by the Circle CLI itself. There is no Circle API
# key. The demo handles login (email + OTP) and stops for you to accept the
# Terms of Use if your account has not. The CLI stores credentials under
# ~/.circle (override with CIRCLE_CLI_HOME).
# https://developers.circle.com/agent-stack
#
# The agent installs Circle's skills into ~/.agents/skills on its first run and
# reads them from there afterwards, so they are shared with every other agent on
# the machine. There is no chain to configure: the CLI settles each payment on a
# chain the seller and your wallet have in common.

# Claude Agent SDK auth. Required. The kit authenticates with an API key only,
# which keeps the spawned Claude Code subprocess non-interactive (the OAuth /
# subscription path can hang it on a login prompt). Get a key at
# https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Optional model override (default: claude-opus-5)
# LLM_MODEL=

# Corporate networks. Uncomment this if your company inspects TLS traffic, so
# the `circle` CLI trusts the certificate authorities in your operating
# system's store. It is additive and drops nothing Node already trusts.
# NODE_OPTIONS=--use-system-ca
#
# This covers the commands the agent runs, which is where the failure normally
# shows up (a bare `fetch failed` from `circle`). It does not cover this kit's
# own calls to your model provider: that process is Bun, and Bun ignores
# NODE_OPTIONS. Start it with `bun --use-system-ca run demo` if that call fails
# too. HTTP_PROXY, HTTPS_PROXY and NO_PROXY are inherited from your shell as
# they are, so there is no need to repeat them here.
